/* Pearson Edexcel GCSE Mathematics (1MA1) — room kits built by hand in a Claude Code session: written by
   Sonnet 5 subagents to the contract in src/kit-validator.js, every question re-solved and the lesson read against
   the specification map by a fresh Opus 5 subagent before shipping. See .claude/skills/course-builder/references/depth.md. */
module.exports = { ID: 'EDX-1MA1', KITS: {"number-structure":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"number-structure","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Every one of the three 1MA1 papers opens on number, and Number carries 22–28% of the Foundation marks. This room holds the machinery the rest of the course runs on: ordering, the four operations, priority of operations, primes and the HCF and LCM, systematic listing, powers and roots, exact answers and standard form. Paper 1 is non-calculator, so the written method and the exact form of the answer are both credited, and a bare answer can lose every method mark. Almost every later topic — ratio, area, probability — is arithmetic wearing a different hat, so a slip here costs marks in rooms that have nothing to do with number.","idea":[{"h":"Ordering numbers and the inequality symbols","t":"Order integers, decimals and fractions by first putting them all in one form, usually decimals, then comparing place value by place value. With negatives, the further a number is from zero the smaller it is, so −7/5 < −1.2. Write the comparison with =, ≠, <, >, ≤ or ≥, reading the symbol as pointing at the smaller number. Examiners ask for a list 'smallest first' or for the right symbol between two values, and a list written in the wrong direction scores nothing.","code":"N1"},{"h":"The four operations and place value","t":"Add, subtract, multiply and divide integers, decimals, proper and improper fractions and mixed numbers, positive and negative, by formal written methods. With decimals, multiply as whole numbers and then count the decimal places: 5.3 × 0.24 has three places altogether, so 1272 becomes 1.272. Subtracting a negative adds. Fractions are added over a common denominator once any mixed number has been written as an improper fraction. On Paper 1 the written method is what carries the method marks.","code":"N2"},{"h":"Inverse operations and priority of operations","t":"Brackets first, then indices (powers and roots), then division and multiplication left to right, then addition and subtraction left to right. A calculator obeys this order too, so the calculation has to be written down correctly in the first place. Each operation is undone by its inverse — + by −, × by ÷, a power by its root — which is how a division is checked and how a calculation is simplified by cancelling before it is worked out.","code":"N3"},{"h":"Primes, factors, multiples, HCF and LCM","t":"A prime has exactly two factors, itself and 1. Every integer above 1 has one prime factorisation and no other — the unique factorisation theorem — written in product notation, for example 720 = 2⁴ × 3² × 5. From two factorisations the HCF is the product of the lowest power of each shared prime and the LCM the product of the highest power of every prime that appears. Exam questions hide these behind contexts: identical bags or rows want the HCF, two events meeting again want the LCM.","code":"N4"},{"h":"Systematic listing strategies","t":"To count every possibility without missing or repeating one, hold one choice fixed and run through the next in a set order, then move the fixed choice on: cheese–tea–flapjack, cheese–tea–brownie, cheese–juice–flapjack, and so on. The marks are for the system, not for the total alone, so the list itself is written out and its order is visible. An unordered scribble that happens to reach the right total is not a complete answer.","code":"N5"},{"h":"Positive integer powers and real roots","t":"A power is repeated multiplication and a root undoes it: ∛64 = 4 because 4³ = 64. Recognise the powers of 2 (2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128), of 3 (3, 9, 27, 81, 243), of 4 (4, 16, 64, 256) and of 5 (5, 25, 125, 625) on sight, because on Paper 1 a question expects them to be written down rather than worked out. The squares up to 15² = 225 and the cubes of 2, 3, 4 and 5 are worth knowing for the same reason.","code":"N6"},{"h":"Calculating with roots and integer indices","t":"Powers of the same base multiply by adding the indices and divide by subtracting them, so 7⁵ × 7³ ÷ 7⁶ = 7⁸ ÷ 7⁶ = 7². A zero index gives 1, and a negative index means the reciprocal, so 5⁻² = 1/5² = 1/25. Roots can be combined within a calculation — √72 ÷ √2 = √36 = 6 — which turns an awkward pair of roots into a whole number. The index form, not a rounded decimal, is usually what the accuracy mark is for.","code":"N7"},{"h":"Exact calculation with fractions and multiples of π","t":"An exact answer is a fraction in its lowest terms or a multiple of π, not a rounded decimal. A circle of radius 6 cm has area π × 6² = 36π cm², and 36π cm² is exact where 113.1 cm² is not. Multiples of π are handled like any other multiple: 3 × 5π = 15π. Paper 1 is non-calculator, so questions there ask for the exact form, and a decimal loses the accuracy mark even when it has been correctly rounded.","code":"N8"},{"h":"Standard form","t":"Standard form is A × 10ⁿ, where A is at least 1 and less than 10 and n is an integer; n is positive for large numbers and negative for small ones, so 0.000 000 065 = 6.5 × 10⁻⁸. To multiply, multiply the A values and add the indices, then adjust the answer back into standard form: 24 × 10¹ is rewritten as 2.4 × 10². To compare two numbers written in standard form, look at the power of 10 first and only then at A.","code":"N9"}],"examples":[{"title":"Comparing signed temperatures","setup":"A weather station logs four overnight temperatures: Monday −2.5 °C, Tuesday −9/4 °C, Wednesday 0.8 °C and Thursday −3/5 °C. Name the warmest night and the coldest night, and then write the correct symbol, < or >, between Monday's reading and Tuesday's reading.","steps":["Change each fraction into a decimal so that all four readings are in one form: −9/4 = −2.25 and −3/5 = −0.6.","Every negative reading is below every positive one, and Wednesday alone is positive, so Wednesday at 0.8 °C was the warmest night.","Of −2.5, −2.25 and −0.6, the reading furthest below zero is −2.5, so Monday was the coldest night.","Monday is −2.5 and Tuesday is −2.25, so Monday's is the smaller; the symbol points at the smaller value, giving −2.5 < −9/4."],"cues":["Are the four readings all written in the same form yet?","How does any negative reading compare with a positive one?","Which position on a thermometer counts as the coldest?","Which value does an inequality symbol point at?"]},{"title":"Checking and undoing with inverse operations","setup":"A stallholder shares a bill of £966 equally between 23 traders and writes down £42 for each of them. Use an inverse operation to decide whether £42 is right, and then find the number that makes 6 × ☐ + 9 = 87 true by undoing the operations in turn.","steps":["The inverse of dividing by 23 is multiplying by 23, so the share is tested with 42 × 23.","42 × 23 = 966, which is the whole bill, so the share of £42 each is right.","In 6 × ☐ + 9 the addition is done last, so it is undone first: 87 − 9 = 78, leaving 6 × ☐ = 78.","Undoing the multiplication as well, 78 ÷ 6 = 13, so the missing number is 13."],"cues":["How can a division be tested without dividing again?","Does the test come back to the whole bill?","Which operation is applied last in 6 × ☐ + 9?","Which operation is still left to undo?"]},{"title":"Prime factorisation, HCF and LCM","setup":"Two numbers are 90 and 126. Without a calculator, write each of them as a product of powers of its prime factors, then use those two factorisations to work out the highest common factor and the lowest common multiple of the pair.","steps":["Divide 90 by primes in turn: 90 = 2 × 45 = 2 × 3 × 15 = 2 × 3 × 3 × 5, so 90 = 2 × 3² × 5.","Do the same for the other: 126 = 2 × 63 = 2 × 3 × 21 = 2 × 3 × 3 × 7, so 126 = 2 × 3² × 7.","HCF: take the lowest power of each prime that is in both lists, 2 and 3², so the HCF = 2 × 9 = 18.","LCM: take the highest power of every prime that appears, 2, 3², 5 and 7, so the LCM = 2 × 9 × 5 × 7 = 630."],"cues":["Which prime is worth trying first on an even number?","Which primes would you expect this second number to share?","For a common factor, is the lower or the higher power needed?","For a common multiple, which primes must be included?"]},{"title":"Listing every possibility in order","setup":"A café meal deal is one sandwich chosen from cheese, ham and tuna, one drink chosen from tea and juice, and one cake chosen from a flapjack and a brownie. By listing the possible deals in a systematic order, work out how many different meal deals there are, and how many of them contain juice.","steps":["Hold cheese and tea, and vary the cake: cheese–tea–flapjack, cheese–tea–brownie.","Keep cheese and move the drink on: cheese–juice–flapjack, cheese–juice–brownie, so cheese gives 4 deals.","Repeat the same four for ham: ham–tea–flapjack, ham–tea–brownie, ham–juice–flapjack, ham–juice–brownie.","Tuna gives four in the same way, so the total is 4 + 4 + 4 = 12 different meal deals.","Juice appears in 2 of the 4 deals for each sandwich, so 2 + 2 + 2 = 6 deals contain juice."],"cues":["Which choice is being held still while another varies?","What has to change next once the cakes are used up?","Why will ham produce the same number as cheese?","How many sandwiches are there to account for?","In each sandwich's list, how many deals had juice?"]},{"title":"Indices and roots without a calculator","setup":"Without a calculator, work out the value of 2⁴ × 2³ ÷ 2⁵ as a whole number, and also the value of √72 ÷ √2.","steps":["The multiplication adds the indices: 2⁴ × 2³ = 2⁷.","The division subtracts the index: 2⁷ ÷ 2⁵ = 2⁷⁻⁵ = 2², which is 4.","For the roots, combine them into a single root: √72 ÷ √2 = √(72 ÷ 2).","Divide inside the root: 72 ÷ 2 = 36, and √36 = 6."],"cues":["What happens to the indices when powers of one base multiply?","And what happens to the indices when they divide?","Can a division of two roots be written as one root?","Is the number now under the root a square number?"]},{"title":"Multiplying in standard form","setup":"A single grain of sand has a mass of 6 × 10⁻⁵ kg. A bucket holds 4 × 10⁶ grains of sand. Without a calculator, work out the total mass of the sand in the bucket, giving your answer in kilograms in standard form.","steps":["Multiply the number parts: 6 × 4 = 24.","Multiply the powers of ten by adding the indices: 10⁻⁵ × 10⁶ = 10¹.","Put the two together: 24 × 10¹, and 24 is not between 1 and 10, so this is not yet standard form.","Rewrite 24 as 2.4 × 10, giving 2.4 × 10 × 10¹ = 2.4 × 10² kg."],"cues":["Which two parts of the numbers can be multiplied separately?","What do you do with indices when powers of ten multiply?","Does the front number obey the rule for standard form?","How does moving the decimal point change the index?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Compare −0.4 and −2/5 using the correct symbol.","a":"−0.4 = −2/5, because −2/5 written as a decimal is −0.4"},{"q":"Work out 20 − 4 × 3 + 2³ without a calculator.","a":"16 (2³ = 8, then 20 − 12 + 8)"},{"q":"Write 48 as a product of powers of its prime factors.","a":"2⁴ × 3"},{"q":"Write 3.6 × 10³ as an ordinary number.","a":"3600"}]},"room":{"facts":["Standard form is A × 10ⁿ with 1 ≤ A < 10 and n an integer; a number smaller than 1 has a negative n.","Laws of indices: aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ, aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ, a⁰ = 1 and a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ.","Priority of operations: brackets, then indices, then division and multiplication left to right, then addition and subtraction left to right.","HCF = the product of the lowest power of each prime the numbers share; LCM = the product of the highest power of every prime that appears in either.","Powers of 2: 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128. Powers of 3: 3, 9, 27, 81, 243. Powers of 4: 4, 16, 64, 256. Powers of 5: 5, 25, 125, 625. Cubes: 2³ = 8, 3³ = 27, 4³ = 64, 5³ = 125.","To multiply in standard form, multiply the A values, add the indices of 10, then adjust so that 1 ≤ A < 10.","Subtracting a negative adds: a − (−b) = a + b; and multiplying two negatives gives a positive."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Write these five numbers in order, starting with the smallest: −1.2, 3/5, −7/5, 0.55, 1/2","a":"−7/5, −1.2, 1/2, 0.55, 3/5","sol":"Write the fractions as decimals: 3/5 = 0.6, −7/5 = −1.4 and 1/2 = 0.5 (M1). The negatives come first and −1.4 < −1.2; then 0.5 < 0.55 < 0.6, so the order is −7/5, −1.2, 1/2, 0.55, 3/5 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Numbers are only easy to compare when they are all written in the same form.","Divide the top of each fraction by the bottom to get a decimal, then compare place value by place value.","Once everything is a decimal, the two negative values are placed first, and the one further from zero is the smaller of them."],"codes":["N1"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out 4.6 × 0.35","a":"1.61","sol":"Multiply as whole numbers by a formal written method: 46 × 35 = 1610 (M1). There is one decimal place in 4.6 and two in 0.35, three altogether, so 1610 becomes 1.61 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The digits of the answer do not depend on where the decimal points are.","Multiply the two numbers as whole numbers first, then decide where the decimal point belongs.","Work out 46 × 35 by a formal written method, then count how many decimal places the two original numbers have between them."],"codes":["N2"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out 30 − 2 × (8 − 3)² ÷ 5","a":"20","sol":"Brackets first: 8 − 3 = 5, then the index: 5² = 25 (M1). Multiplication and division left to right: 2 × 25 = 50 and 50 ÷ 5 = 10; the subtraction is last, 30 − 10 = 20 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["There is a fixed order for these operations, and it is not simply left to right.","Brackets, then the power, then the multiplication and division left to right, and the subtraction last of all.","Evaluate the bracket first, and square the result of that before anything else happens."],"codes":["N3"]},{"q":"Write 84 as a product of powers of its prime factors. A calculator may be used.","a":"2² × 3 × 7","sol":"Divide by primes in turn: 84 = 2 × 42 = 2 × 2 × 21 = 2 × 2 × 3 × 7 (M1). Collecting the repeated prime in product notation gives 84 = 2² × 3 × 7 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Every whole number above 1 is built from primes in exactly one way.","Divide by the smallest prime that goes in exactly, then keep dividing each quotient by a prime until you reach 1.","84 is even, so begin by dividing by 2 and carry on from the quotient; collect any repeated prime as a power at the end."],"codes":["N4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out the value of 2⁵ ÷ 2³ + √81 − ∛27","a":"10","sol":"Divide the powers by subtracting the indices: 2⁵ ÷ 2³ = 2² = 4 (M1). √81 = 9 and ∛27 = 3 (B1). Adding and subtracting left to right, 4 + 9 − 3 = 10 (A1).","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The two powers share a base, so that division can be done in index form.","Divide the powers by subtracting the indices, work out each root, then add and subtract left to right.","2⁵ ÷ 2³ becomes a single power of 2; √81 asks which number squared gives 81, and ∛27 which number cubed gives 27."],"codes":["N6","N7"]},{"q":"A virus particle measures 0.000 000 12 m across. Write this measurement in standard form. A calculator may be used.","a":"1.2 × 10⁻⁷ m","sol":"The digits give a number between 1 and 10: 1.2 (M1). The decimal point has to move 7 places to the right from 0.000 000 12 to reach 1.2, and the measurement is smaller than 1, so n = −7 and the answer is 1.2 × 10⁻⁷ m (A1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Standard form needs exactly one non-zero digit in front of the decimal point.","Write the digits as a number between 1 and 10, then count how many places the decimal point has to move.","Count the places between the decimal point in the measurement and the position just after its first non-zero digit; a number smaller than 1 takes a negative index."],"codes":["N9"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out 4⁻², giving your answer as a fraction in its lowest terms.","a":"1/16","sol":"A negative index means the reciprocal of the matching positive power: 4⁻² = 1/4² (M1). 4² = 16, so 4⁻² = 1/16 (A1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["A negative index does not make the answer a negative number.","A negative index means take the reciprocal of the matching positive power.","Rewrite the expression as 1 divided by the matching positive power of 4, then evaluate that power."],"codes":["N7"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Ali makes two-digit numbers using two different digits chosen from 3, 5 and 7. By listing them in a systematic order, find (a) how many such two-digit numbers there are, and (b) the largest of them.","a":"(a) 6   (b) 75","sol":"Hold the tens digit and vary the units digit: with 3 in the tens, 35 and 37 (M1). With 5 in the tens, 53 and 57; with 7 in the tens, 73 and 75 (M1). The list is complete with no repeats, so (a) there are 6 numbers and (b) the largest is 75 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Each number uses two different digits, so no digit may be repeated within it.","Hold the tens digit still and run through the possible units digits, then move the tens digit on.","Write down the numbers whose tens digit is the smallest of the three first, then those with the next tens digit, and so on."],"codes":["N5"]},{"q":"Buses to Leeds leave a stop every 12 minutes and buses to York leave the same stop every 18 minutes. Both leave together at 09:00. Work out the time at which they next leave together. A calculator may be used.","a":"09:36","sol":"They leave together again after a number of minutes that is a multiple of both gaps, so the lowest common multiple is needed (M1). 12 = 2² × 3 and 18 = 2 × 3², so the LCM = 2² × 3² = 36 minutes (A1). 09:00 plus 36 minutes is 09:36 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["They meet again at a time that is a whole number of gaps along both routes.","Find the lowest common multiple of the two gaps in minutes, then add it to the starting time.","Write each gap as a product of prime powers, then take the highest power of every prime that appears."],"codes":["N4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A circle has a radius of 7 cm. Work out its area, giving your answer as a multiple of π. The formulae sheet printed with the paper gives area of a circle = πr².","a":"49π cm²","sol":"Substitute r = 7 into the printed formula: area = π × 7² (M1). 7² = 49, so the exact area is 49π cm² (A1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The formulae sheet printed with the paper already gives the area of a circle.","Substitute the radius into πr² and leave π in the answer instead of multiplying it out.","Square the radius first, then write that result in front of π with the correct square unit."],"codes":["N8"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out 2/5 + 1 1/4, giving your answer as a mixed number in its lowest terms.","a":"1 13/20","sol":"Write the mixed number as an improper fraction: 1 1/4 = 5/4 (B1). Use the common denominator 20: 2/5 = 8/20 and 5/4 = 25/20 (M1). 8/20 + 25/20 = 33/20 = 1 13/20 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A mixed number is much easier to add once it is written as a single fraction.","Change the mixed number to an improper fraction, then rewrite both fractions over a common denominator.","The denominators are 5 and 4, so use their lowest common multiple as the common denominator."],"codes":["N2","N8"]},{"q":"A gardener has 60 tulip bulbs and 84 daffodil bulbs. She plants them all in identical rows, every row holding the same number of tulips and the same number of daffodils, with none left over, and she wants as many rows as possible. Work out (a) the number of rows she makes and (b) the number of tulip bulbs in each row. A calculator may be used.","a":"(a) 12 rows   (b) 5 tulip bulbs","sol":"The number of rows must divide both 60 and 84 exactly, so the greatest possible number of rows is their highest common factor (M1). 60 = 2² × 3 × 5 and 84 = 2² × 3 × 7 (M1). The lowest power of each shared prime gives HCF = 2² × 3 = 12, so (a) she makes 12 rows (A1). Each row then holds 60 ÷ 12 = 5 tulip bulbs (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Every row is identical and no bulbs at all are left over.","The number of rows has to divide both totals exactly, and the greatest number that does is the highest common factor.","Write both totals as products of prime powers, then take the lowest power of each prime they share."],"codes":["N4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Ellie has a £2 coin, a 50p coin, a 20p coin and a 10p coin. She chooses exactly two of the four coins. By listing the possible pairs in a systematic order, work out (a) how many different pairs she can choose and (b) the total value of the most valuable pair.","a":"(a) 6 pairs   (b) £2.50","sol":"Hold the £2 coin and pair it with each of the others: £2 and 50p, £2 and 20p, £2 and 10p (M1). Then hold the 50p: 50p and 20p, 50p and 10p; then the 20p: 20p and 10p (M1). The list has no repeats, so (a) 3 + 2 + 1 = 6 different pairs (A1). The most valuable pair is the two largest coins, £2 + 50p = £2.50 (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Two coins are chosen from four, and the order in which she picks them up does not matter.","Hold the most valuable coin and pair it with each of the others in turn, then move on to the next coin down.","List the pairs that contain the £2 coin first, then the pairs that contain the 50p coin but not the £2 coin, and so on."],"codes":["N5","N2"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Three stars are 4.1 × 10¹³ km, 9.5 × 10¹² km and 3.8 × 10¹³ km from Earth. Work out (a) the three distances in order, nearest first, and (b) how much further away the furthest star is than the nearest, in km in standard form.","a":"(a) 9.5 × 10¹² km, 3.8 × 10¹³ km, 4.1 × 10¹³ km   (b) 3.15 × 10¹³ km","sol":"Compare the powers of 10 first: 10¹² is smaller than 10¹³, so 9.5 × 10¹² is the nearest; among the other two, 3.8 < 4.1 (M1). So (a) the order is 9.5 × 10¹² km, 3.8 × 10¹³ km, 4.1 × 10¹³ km (A1). To subtract, write both with the same power of 10: 9.5 × 10¹² = 0.95 × 10¹³ (M1). Then 4.1 × 10¹³ − 0.95 × 10¹³ = 3.15 × 10¹³ km (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["In standard form the power of 10 decides the size before the number in front of it does.","Compare the indices first, and only compare the front numbers when the indices are equal.","For the subtraction, rewrite the smaller distance so that it has the same power of 10 as the larger one."],"codes":["N9","N1"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A number n is given by n = 2³ × 3² × 5. Work out (a) the value of n and (b) the smallest whole number that n must be multiplied by so that the product is a square number.","a":"(a) 360   (b) 10","sol":"Evaluate the powers: 2³ = 8 and 3² = 9, so (a) n = 8 × 9 × 5 = 360 (B1). A square number has an even index for every prime in its factorisation, and here the indices of 2 and of 5 are odd (M1). Multiplying by 2 × 5 gives 2⁴ × 3² × 5² = 3600 = 60², so (b) the smallest multiplier is 10 (A1).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["A square number is one whose square root is a whole number.","Evaluate the powers to get n, then look at whether each index in the factorisation is even or odd.","In 2³ × 3² × 5 the indices are 3, 2 and 1, and a square number needs every index to be even."],"codes":["N4","N6"]},{"q":"A shop sells pens in boxes of 48. A school orders 25 boxes at £6.40 for each box and pays £20 for delivery. Work out the cost of one pen, in pence. A calculator may be used.","a":"15p","sol":"Cost of the boxes: 25 × £6.40 = £160 (M1). Total cost including delivery: £160 + £20 = £180 (A1). Number of pens: 25 × 48 = 1200 (M1). Cost of one pen: £180 ÷ 1200 = £0.15 (M1), which is 15p (A1).","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["The delivery charge is paid once for the whole order, not once for each box.","Work out the cost of the boxes, add the delivery charge, then divide by the total number of pens.","Multiply the number of boxes by the price of one box; separately, multiply the number of boxes by the number of pens in a box."],"codes":["N2"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write 4.05 × 10⁴ as an ordinary number.","a":"40 500"},{"q":"Work out 6 + 4 × 5²","a":"106"},{"q":"Write down the lowest common multiple of 6 and 8.","a":"24"},{"q":"Work out 2⁻³, giving your answer as a fraction.","a":"1/8"}]},"cards":[{"front":"The inequality symbols =, ≠, <, >, ≤, ≥","back":"= equals; ≠ is not equal to; < is less than; > is greater than; ≤ is less than or equal to; ≥ is greater than or equal to. The symbol always points at the smaller value.","code":"N1","open":false},{"front":"Ordering negative numbers","back":"The further a negative number lies from zero, the smaller it is, so −7 < −3 < −0.5 < 0 < 0.5. Every negative number is smaller than every positive one.","code":"N1","open":false},{"front":"Name two ways of showing that 0.6 is greater than 4/7","back":"Write both as decimals, since 4/7 = 0.571… and 0.571… < 0.6; write both over the common denominator 35, since 0.6 = 21/35 and 4/7 = 20/35; multiply out, since 0.6 × 7 = 4.2 and 4.2 > 4.","code":"N1","open":true},{"front":"Place value when multiplying decimals","back":"Multiply the digits as whole numbers, then give the answer as many decimal places as the two numbers have between them: 0.3 × 0.04 = 0.012.","code":"N2","open":false},{"front":"Subtracting a negative number","back":"Subtracting a negative is the same as adding: −7 − (−12) = −7 + 12 = 5. Two negative signs next to each other become a plus.","code":"N2","open":false},{"front":"Priority of operations (BIDMAS)","back":"Brackets, then indices (powers and roots), then division and multiplication left to right, then addition and subtraction left to right.","code":"N3","open":false},{"front":"Inverse operations","back":"Each operation is undone by its inverse: + and −, × and ÷, a power and its root. Use the inverse to check: 372 ÷ 12 = 31 because 31 × 12 = 372.","code":"N3","open":false},{"front":"Prime number","back":"A whole number greater than 1 with exactly two factors, itself and 1. The primes begin 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19. 1 is not prime.","code":"N4","open":false},{"front":"Prime factorisation and the unique factorisation theorem","back":"Every integer greater than 1 is a product of primes in exactly one way, written in product notation: 720 = 2⁴ × 3² × 5.","code":"N4","open":false},{"front":"Reading the HCF and the LCM from two prime factorisations","back":"HCF: multiply the lowest power of each prime that appears in both. LCM: multiply the highest power of every prime that appears in either.","code":"N4","open":false},{"front":"A systematic listing strategy","back":"Hold one choice fixed and vary the next in a set order, then move the fixed choice on. The list is then complete, with no gaps and no repeats.","code":"N5","open":false},{"front":"The powers and roots to recognise on sight","back":"2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128; 3, 9, 27, 81, 243; 4, 16, 64, 256; 5, 25, 125, 625. A root undoes its power: ∛64 = 4 because 4³ = 64.","code":"N6","open":false},{"front":"The laws of indices for integer indices","back":"aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ; aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ; a⁰ = 1; a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ. The base must be the same before indices may be added or subtracted.","code":"N7","open":false},{"front":"An exact answer","back":"A fraction in its lowest terms or a multiple of π, never a rounded decimal: a circle of radius 3 cm has area 9π cm², not 28.3 cm².","code":"N8","open":false},{"front":"Standard form A × 10ⁿ","back":"A is at least 1 and less than 10, and n is an integer: positive for large numbers, negative for small ones. 0.000 45 = 4.5 × 10⁻⁴.","code":"N9","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: area of a circle = πr², where r is the radius — so an exact area such as 49π cm² can be written straight from the sheet.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd, where r is the radius and d is the diameter.","Those two circle results are the only things on this room's syllabus that the Foundation Exam Aid prints; everything below has to be remembered.","Remember: the laws of indices — aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ, aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ, a⁰ = 1, a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ (N7). The Exam Aid does not print them at either tier.","Remember: standard form is A × 10ⁿ with 1 ≤ A < 10 and n an integer, and to multiply you multiply the A values, add the indices of 10, then adjust A back into range (N9).","Remember: HCF = the product of the lowest power of each prime shared by the two factorisations; LCM = the product of the highest power of every prime that appears (N4).","Remember: the priority of operations — brackets, indices, division and multiplication left to right, then addition and subtraction left to right (N3).","Remember: the squares up to 15² = 225, the cubes 2³ = 8, 3³ = 27, 4³ = 64 and 5³ = 125, and the powers of 2 up to 2⁷ = 128 (N6).","Remember: when multiplying decimals the answer carries as many decimal places as the two numbers have between them, and a − (−b) = a + b (N2).","For reference, the rest of the Foundation Exam Aid — none of it needed in this room — is the area of a trapezium, the volume of a prism, Pythagoras' theorem, the three trigonometric ratios, the compound interest formula and P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B)."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T11:42:40Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.82,"notes":"Every answer key is correct — all 16 bank questions, the 4 exit questions, the 4 lesson checks and all 6 worked examples re-solved independently in Python, with mark tallies matching each tariff and the Formulae sheet matching the 2026 Foundation Exam Aid word for word — so the only work before publishing is to stop lesson.idea from printing the answers to questions 1, 5, 6 and 9."}}},"number-fdp":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"number-fdp","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Fractions, decimals and percentages are the working language of the rest of this course. A sale price, a share of a ratio, a rate of pay, a probability and a share of a survey are all the same move: one quantity acted on by a multiplier. This room gives you the three conversions that unlock that move — a terminating decimal into its fraction and back, a ratio into the fraction of the whole, and a fraction or percentage used as an operator. Paper 1 is taken without a calculator, so these conversions have to be done by hand and answers left exact, as a fraction in its lowest terms; on Papers 2 and 3 the same multipliers are what you key in. Get them wrong here and every later percentage, proportion and rate question goes wrong with them.","idea":[{"h":"Terminating decimals and their fractions","t":"A terminating decimal stops, so its last digit names a denominator: tenths, hundredths, thousandths. Write the digits over that place value and cancel by the highest common factor — 0.28 = 28/100 = 7/25, and 1.6 = 16/10 = 8/5. To go back, divide numerator by denominator: 9/40 = 9 ÷ 40 = 0.225. Examiners ask for this in three shapes: convert and give the fraction in its lowest terms, convert a fraction to a decimal, or order a mixed list of fractions and decimals by putting them all into the same form first. Knowing 1/2, 1/4, 3/4, 1/5, 1/8 and 1/20 by sight saves the division every time.","code":"N10"},{"h":"Fractions hiding inside a ratio","t":"A ratio counts equal parts, so a ratio a : b describes a + b parts altogether and each quantity is a fraction of that whole: 2 : 3 makes 2/5 and 3/5. This is why a ratio question and a fraction question can be answered by the same working — find the total number of parts, then take that many parts of the amount. The move also runs backwards: if 3/7 of a class walk to school, the rest are 4/7 and the ratio walk : bus is 3 : 4. Watch for part : part against part : whole, because 3 : 4 and 3/7 describe the same class.","code":"N11"},{"h":"Fractions and percentages as operators","t":"An operator is something that acts on a quantity and scales it. The word 'of' means multiply: 3/4 of 60 kg = 3/4 × 60 = 45 kg, and 15% of 240 = 0.15 × 240 = 36. A change is the same idea in one step: an increase of p% leaves you with 100% + p%, so the multiplier is 1 + p/100 (15% gives × 1.15), and a decrease leaves 100% − p%, so the multiplier is 1 − p/100 (35% gives × 0.65). One multiplication, not two steps, is what the mark scheme looks for, and by hand it is often easier as a fraction: × 0.75 is × 3/4.","code":"N12"}],"examples":[{"title":"A terminating decimal written as a fraction","setup":"A bolt is 0.28 m long. Write 0.28 as a fraction in its lowest terms.","steps":["The last digit 8 sits in the hundredths column, so 0.28 = 28/100.","Both parts divide by 4: 28 ÷ 4 = 7 and 100 ÷ 4 = 25.","So 0.28 = 7/25, and 7 and 25 share no common factor, so this is lowest terms."],"cues":["Which place value does the last digit sit in?","What is the highest common factor of 28 and 100?","Can the cancelled fraction be reduced any further?"]},{"title":"Comparing a fraction with a decimal","setup":"Two ribbons are measured in different ways. Ribbon A is 7/8 of a metre long. Ribbon B is 0.87 m long. Which ribbon is longer, and by how many metres?","steps":["Change Ribbon A into a decimal by short division: 7 ÷ 8 = 0.875.","Pad Ribbon B to the same number of decimal places: 0.87 becomes 0.870.","Digit by digit the tenths and hundredths match, but in the thousandths 5 is bigger than 0, so 0.875 is the larger value.","0.875 − 0.870 = 0.005, so Ribbon A is longer, by 0.005 m."],"cues":["Which form should both lengths be in before they are compared?","Why is it safer to give both lengths the same number of decimal places?","Which column decides the comparison here?","How is the gap between two decimals found?"]},{"title":"From a ratio to a fraction of the whole","setup":"A fruit bowl holds only apples and pears, in the ratio 4 : 5. There are 36 pieces of fruit in the bowl altogether. What fraction of the fruit are apples, and how many apples are there?","steps":["Add the parts: 4 + 5 = 9, so the fruit is shared into 9 equal parts.","Apples take 4 of those 9 parts, so the fraction that are apples is 4/9, and that fraction acts on the 36 pieces of fruit.","One part is 36 ÷ 9 = 4 pieces of fruit, so 4/9 of the bowl is 4 × 4 = 16 apples."],"cues":["How many equal parts does this ratio describe?","How many of those parts are apples, and what does that fraction act on?","How many pieces of fruit make up one part?"]},{"title":"A percentage increase in one multiplication","setup":"A train ticket costs £48. The price is to be increased by 15%. Use a multiplier to work out the new price.","steps":["An increase of 15% keeps the whole 100% and adds 15% on top, so the multiplier is 1 + 15/100 = 1.15.","New price = old price × multiplier = 48 × 1.15.","48 × 1.15 = 48 + 4.80 + 2.40 = 55.20, so the new price is £55.20."],"cues":["What percentage of the old price is the new price?","Which single calculation now gives the new price?","What are 10% and 5% of £48?"]},{"title":"A percentage decrease done as a fraction","setup":"A coat costs £84. In a sale, every price is reduced by 25%. Work out the sale price, using a multiplier written as a fraction in its lowest terms.","steps":["Taking 25% off leaves 100% − 25% = 75% of the price, so the multiplier is 0.75.","As a fraction 0.75 = 75/100 = 3/4 after dividing both parts by 25, so the sale price is 84 × 3/4.","84 ÷ 4 = 21 and 21 × 3 = 63, so the sale price is £63."],"cues":["What percentage of the price does the shopper actually pay?","How could this multiplier be written so the arithmetic is easy by hand?","What is one quarter of £84?"]},{"title":"From a fraction back to a ratio","setup":"In a class of 28 students, 3/7 walk to school and the rest travel by bus. Write the number who walk to the number who travel by bus as a ratio in its simplest form, and work out how many students are in each group.","steps":["Subtract from the whole: 7/7 − 3/7 = 4/7 do not walk, giving walk : bus = 3 : 4.","One seventh of the class is 28 ÷ 7 = 4 students, so 3/7 of 28 = 4 × 3 = 12 walk.","28 − 12 = 16 travel by bus, and 12 : 16 divided through by 4 is 3 : 4, which agrees with the ratio."],"cues":["What fraction of the class does not walk?","How many students make up one seventh of the class?","How can the two group sizes be used to check the ratio?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Write 0.4 as a fraction in its lowest terms.","a":"2/5"},{"q":"Write 7/20 as a decimal.","a":"0.35"},{"q":"Counters are red : blue in the ratio 3 : 5. What fraction of the counters are blue?","a":"5/8"},{"q":"Work out 2/3 of 54 m.","a":"36 m"},{"q":"Write down the single multiplier that increases an amount by 22%.","a":"1.22"}]},"room":{"facts":["Decimal to fraction: put the digits over the place value of the last digit, then cancel — 0.28 = 28/100 = 7/25.","Fraction to decimal: divide the numerator by the denominator — 9/40 = 9 ÷ 40 = 0.225.","A ratio a : b has a + b equal parts, so the first quantity is a/(a + b) of the whole and the second is b/(a + b).","A fraction of the whole gives a ratio: if a/b have the property, property : rest = a : (b − a).","'Of' means multiply: a/b of Q = a/b × Q, and p% of Q = (p/100) × Q.","Multiplier for an increase of p% is 1 + p/100; for a decrease of p% it is 1 − p/100 (a 35% decrease is × 0.65).","Equivalents to know by sight: 1/2 = 0.5, 1/4 = 0.25, 3/4 = 0.75, 1/5 = 0.2, 1/8 = 0.125, 3/8 = 0.375, 1/20 = 0.05, 1/25 = 0.04."],"questions":[{"q":"Write 1.6 as an improper fraction in its lowest terms. (non-calculator)","a":"8/5","sol":"The last digit sits in the tenths column, so 1.6 = 16/10. M1 for 16/10 (or for 1 whole and 6/10). Both parts divide by 2: 16 ÷ 2 = 8 and 10 ÷ 2 = 5, giving 8/5. A1 for 8/5 (16/10 uncancelled scores only the method mark).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The digit after the point tells you which place value names the denominator.","Write all the digits over that place value, then cancel by the highest common factor.","The first line is 1.6 = 16/10; the cancelling comes after it."],"codes":["N10"]},{"q":"Write 9/40 as a decimal. (non-calculator)","a":"0.225","sol":"9/40 means 9 ÷ 40. Short division gives 9.000 ÷ 40 = 0.225 (or multiply both parts by 25: 9/40 = 225/1000 = 0.225). B1 for 0.225.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The line in a fraction is a division sign.","Divide the numerator by the denominator, or scale the denominator up to a power of 10.","The first line is 9 ÷ 40, worked as 9.000 ÷ 40."],"codes":["N10"]},{"q":"Work out 3/5 of 85 kg. (non-calculator)","a":"51 kg","sol":"'Of' means multiply, so 3/5 of 85 = 3/5 × 85. One fifth is 85 ÷ 5 = 17, so three fifths is 17 × 3. M1 for 85 ÷ 5 = 17 or for 3/5 × 85 seen. A1 for 51 kg, with the unit.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A fraction in front of a quantity is acting on it.","Divide by the denominator to get one part, then multiply by the numerator.","The first line is 85 ÷ 5, and the numerator is used after that."],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"Write down the single number an amount is multiplied by to increase it by 7%. (non-calculator)","a":"1.07","sol":"After a 7% increase the amount is 100% + 7% = 107% of what it was, and 107% = 107/100. B1 for 1.07.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["After the increase, what percentage of the original amount is left standing?","A percentage only works as a multiplier once it is written as a decimal.","The same route on different numbers: a 9% increase gives 100% + 9% = 109%, so the multiplier is 1.09."],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"A shelf holds fiction and non-fiction books in the ratio 6 : 5. What fraction of the books on the shelf are non-fiction? Give your answer in its simplest form. (non-calculator)","a":"5/11","sol":"The ratio splits the books into 6 + 5 = 11 equal parts. M1 for 11 parts seen. Non-fiction takes 5 of those 11 parts, so the fraction is 5/11. A1 for 5/11.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A ratio counts equal parts, and the whole shelf is all of them.","Add the parts to get the whole, then count the parts you want.","The first line is 6 + 5, and the non-fiction parts go on top."],"codes":["N11"]},{"q":"Priya is paid £9.60 per hour. Her rate of pay is increased by 5%. Work out her new rate of pay per hour. (a calculator may be used)","a":"£10.08","sol":"An increase of 5% gives 100% + 5% = 105%, so the multiplier is 1.05. New rate = 9.60 × 1.05 = 10.08. M1 for 1.05 or for 9.60 × 1.05 (or for 5% of 9.60 = 0.48). A1 for £10.08.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["One multiplication can do the whole increase.","Work out the multiplier for a 5% increase, then apply it to the old rate.","The first line is old rate × multiplier, with the multiplier found from 100% + 5%."],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"In a car park, 0.45 of the vehicles are red. Write the ratio of red vehicles to vehicles that are not red, in its simplest form. (non-calculator)","a":"9 : 11","sol":"0.45 = 45/100 = 9/20 of the vehicles are red. M1 for 45/100 or 9/20. The rest are 20/20 − 9/20 = 11/20 (or 1 − 0.45 = 0.55 = 11/20). M1 for 11/20 or 0.55. In twentieths the vehicles split 9 parts to 11 parts, so red : not red = 9 : 11. A1 for 9 : 11.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A decimal of the whole can be read as a fraction of the whole.","Turn the decimal into a fraction in its lowest terms, then find the fraction that is left over.","The first line is 0.45 = 45/100, and the two fractions over the same denominator give the ratio."],"codes":["N10","N11"]},{"q":"A laptop costs £540. In a sale the price is reduced by 35%. Work out the sale price. (a calculator may be used)","a":"£351","sol":"A reduction of 35% leaves 100% − 35% = 65%, so the multiplier is 0.65. M1 for 0.65 (or for 35% of 540 = 189). Sale price = 540 × 0.65. M1 for 540 × 0.65 (or 540 − 189). This gives 351. A1 for £351.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The shopper still pays for most of the laptop — how much of it?","Find the percentage that is left and use it as a multiplier on the old price.","The first line is 100% − 35%, written as a decimal multiplier."],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"A jar contains only 20p coins and 50p coins, in the ratio 3 : 2. There are 45 coins in the jar. How many 50p coins are in the jar? (non-calculator)","a":"18","sol":"The ratio gives 3 + 2 = 5 equal parts, so the 50p coins are 2/5 of the coins. M1 for 5 parts or for the fraction 2/5. One fifth of the jar is 45 ÷ 5 = 9 coins. M1 for 45 ÷ 5 = 9. So 2/5 of 45 = 9 × 2 = 18. A1 for 18.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The 50p coins are a fraction of all the coins, not of the 20p coins.","Turn the ratio into the fraction that is 50p coins, then take that fraction of 45.","The first line is 3 + 2 = 5 parts, and 45 is then divided by the number of parts."],"codes":["N11","N12"]},{"q":"A café bakes 120 cakes. It sells 5/8 of them in the morning, then sells 40% of the cakes that are left during the afternoon. How many cakes are unsold at the end of the day? (a calculator may be used)","a":"27","sol":"Morning: 5/8 of 120 = (120 ÷ 8) × 5 = 75 cakes sold. M1 for 5/8 × 120 = 75. Left at midday: 120 − 75 = 45. M1 for 45. Afternoon: 40% are sold, so 60% of the 45 are left, giving 0.6 × 45. M1 for 0.6 × 45 (or 0.4 × 45 = 18 followed by 45 − 18). This gives 27. A1 for 27 cakes.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The afternoon percentage acts on what is left, not on the whole batch.","Work out the morning sales first, subtract them, then apply a multiplier to what remains.","The first line is 5/8 × 120, and the remainder is what the afternoon multiplier acts on."],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"Put these four numbers in order, smallest first: 0.7, 5/8, 0.66, 17/25. (non-calculator)","a":"5/8, 0.66, 17/25, 0.7","sol":"Put everything in one form. 5/8 = 5 ÷ 8 = 0.625. M1 for 0.625. 17/25 = 68/100 = 0.68. M1 for 0.68. Comparing 0.625, 0.66, 0.68 and 0.7 gives 0.625 < 0.66 < 0.68 < 0.7, so in the forms given the order is 5/8, 0.66, 17/25, 0.7. A1 for the correct order, written in the original forms.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Numbers can only be compared when they are written the same way.","Convert both fractions to decimals, then compare place value by place value.","The first line is 5 ÷ 8, and the second fraction is scaled to hundredths."],"codes":["N10"]},{"q":"Ali and Ben share a piece of work. Ali does 0.375 of the work and Ben does the rest. They are paid £560 for the job, shared between them in the same proportions as the work. How much is Ben paid? (non-calculator)","a":"£350","sol":"Ali: 0.375 = 375/1000 = 3/8 of the work. M1 for 3/8. Ben does the rest, 1 − 3/8 = 5/8 (so Ali : Ben = 3 : 5). M1 for 5/8 or for the ratio 3 : 5. Ben's pay = 5/8 of 560, and 560 ÷ 8 = 70. M1 for 560 ÷ 8 = 70. So 70 × 5 = 350. A1 for £350.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The decimal tells you Ali's share of one whole job.","Turn the decimal into a fraction, take it from the whole to get Ben's fraction, then apply that fraction to the money.","The first line is 0.375 = 375/1000, cancelled, and the whole is 1."],"codes":["N10","N11","N12"]},{"q":"A shop buys 240 bottles of juice for 90p each. It sells 3/4 of the bottles at £1.50 each, and sells the rest at a price that is 30% less than £1.50. Work out the shop's total profit on the 240 bottles. (a calculator may be used)","a":"£117","sol":"Cost = 240 × 0.90 = £216. M1 for 240 × 0.90. Bottles at full price = 3/4 × 240 = 180, so 240 − 180 = 60 are left. M1 for 180 and 60. Full-price takings = 180 × 1.50 = £270. M1 for 270. The reduced price is 1.50 × 0.7 = £1.05, so the last bottles take 60 × 1.05 = £63. M1 for 63. Total takings = 270 + 63 = £333, and profit = 333 − 216 = £117. A1 for £117.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Profit compares what the shop takes in with what it paid out.","Work out the cost, then the takings from each group of bottles separately, using a multiplier for the reduced price.","The first line is 240 × 0.90 for the cost, and the bottles then split 3/4 to the rest."],"codes":["N12"]},{"q":"A youth club has 96 members. The ratio of members aged under 16 to members aged 16 or over is 5 : 3. A quarter of the under-16 members are girls, and 1/3 of the members aged 16 or over are girls. How many members of the club are girls? (non-calculator)","a":"27","sol":"The ratio gives 5 + 3 = 8 equal parts, so the under-16 members are 5/8 of the club and the rest are 3/8. M1 for 5/8 (or 8 parts with 96 ÷ 8 = 12). Under 16: 5/8 of 96 = (96 ÷ 8) × 5 = 60 members; 16 or over: 96 − 60 = 36 members. M1 for 60 and 36. Girls under 16 = 1/4 of 60 = 15. M1 for 15. Girls aged 16 or over = 1/3 of 36 = 12. M1 for 12. Total girls = 15 + 12 = 27. A1 for 27.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["The ratio splits the club before either fraction is used.","Find the size of each age group first, then apply each group's own fraction to that group.","The first line is 5 + 3 parts, giving the fraction of the club that is under 16."],"codes":["N11","N12"]},{"q":"The same pair of trainers is on offer in two shops. In shop A they cost £80 and the price is reduced by 1/4. In shop B they cost £76 and the price is reduced by 20%. Which shop sells the trainers more cheaply, and by how much? (non-calculator)","a":"Shop A, by £0.80","sol":"Shop A: a reduction of 1/4 leaves 3/4, so the price is 80 × 3/4. M1 for 80 × 3/4 (or 1/4 of 80 = 20 taken off). Shop B: a reduction of 20% leaves 80%, so the price is 76 × 0.8. M1 for 76 × 0.8 (or 20% of 76 = 15.20 taken off). The prices are £60 and £60.80. A1 for both £60 and £60.80. 60.80 − 60 = 0.80, so shop A is cheaper by £0.80. A1 for the comparison with the difference.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A fraction off and a percentage off are the same kind of instruction.","Turn each reduction into the fraction or decimal of the price that is still paid, then compare the two sale prices.","The first line is 80 × 3/4 for shop A, with shop B needing its own multiplier."],"codes":["N10","N12"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write 0.24 as a fraction in its lowest terms.","a":"6/25"},{"q":"Write 3/16 as a decimal.","a":"0.1875"},{"q":"Sweets are red : green in the ratio 2 : 7. What fraction of the sweets are green?","a":"7/9"},{"q":"Write down the single multiplier that decreases an amount by 18%.","a":"0.82"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Terminating decimal","back":"A decimal that stops after a fixed number of decimal places, such as 0.375. Written over a power of 10 it cancels, so in lowest terms its denominator has no prime factors other than 2 and 5: 0.28 = 7/25.","code":"N10","open":false},{"front":"Decimal to fraction: the method","back":"Write the digits over the place value of the last digit (tenths, hundredths, thousandths), then cancel by the highest common factor: 0.28 = 28/100 = 7/25.","code":"N10","open":false},{"front":"Fraction to decimal: the method","back":"Divide the numerator by the denominator: 3/8 = 3 ÷ 8 = 0.375. Scaling to a power of 10 also works: 9/40 = 225/1000 = 0.225.","code":"N10","open":false},{"front":"3.5 written as an improper fraction","back":"3.5 = 35/10 = 7/2. The 3 is 30/10 and the 0.5 is 5/10, and 35/10 cancels by 5.","code":"N10","open":false},{"front":"The decimal equivalents worth knowing by sight","back":"1/2 = 0.5, 1/4 = 0.25, 3/4 = 0.75, 1/5 = 0.2, 1/8 = 0.125, 3/8 = 0.375, 1/10 = 0.1, 1/20 = 0.05, 1/25 = 0.04, 1/40 = 0.025.","code":"N10","open":false},{"front":"Give one example of a terminating decimal written as a fraction in its lowest terms","back":"0.45 = 9/20; 0.6 = 3/5; 0.125 = 1/8. Any decimal correctly converted and fully cancelled is acceptable.","code":"N10","open":true},{"front":"Number of parts in the ratio a : b","back":"a + b equal parts. One part is the whole amount divided by a + b, which is the step most ratio questions turn on.","code":"N11","open":false},{"front":"Fraction of the whole from a ratio","back":"In the ratio a : b the first quantity is a/(a + b) of the whole and the second is b/(a + b). So 2 : 3 gives 2/5 and 3/5.","code":"N11","open":false},{"front":"Ratio from a fraction of the whole","back":"If a/b of a set has a property, the rest is (b − a)/b, so property : rest = a : (b − a). 3/7 of a class gives 3 : 4.","code":"N11","open":false},{"front":"Part : part against part : whole","back":"3 : 4 counts one group against the other; 3/7 counts one group against everybody. Check which the question gives before using it.","code":"N11","open":false},{"front":"Fraction as an operator","back":"'Of' means multiply: a/b of Q = a/b × Q. Divide by the denominator for one part, then multiply by the numerator: 3/4 of 60 kg = 45 kg.","code":"N12","open":false},{"front":"Percentage as an operator","back":"p% of Q = (p/100) × Q. So 15% of 240 = 0.15 × 240 = 36.","code":"N12","open":false},{"front":"Multiplier for an increase of p%","back":"1 + p/100. An increase of 15% is × 1.15, because the new amount is 115% of the old one.","code":"N12","open":false},{"front":"Multiplier for a decrease of p%","back":"1 − p/100. A decrease of 35% is × 0.65, because 65% of the amount is left.","code":"N12","open":false},{"front":"Multiplier written as a fraction","back":"Any multiplier can be a fraction: × 0.75 is × 3/4 and × 1.2 is × 6/5. Without a calculator the fraction is usually the quicker route.","code":"N12","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Decimal to fraction: digits over the place value of the last digit, then cancel — 0.28 = 28/100 = 7/25. Not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","Fraction to decimal: numerator ÷ denominator — 9/40 = 9 ÷ 40 = 0.225. Not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","Decimal to improper fraction and back: 3.5 = 35/10 = 7/2. Not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","Fraction of the whole from a ratio a : b — first quantity = a/(a + b), second = b/(a + b), and one part = whole ÷ (a + b). Not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","Ratio from a fraction — if a/b of a set has a property, property : rest = a : (b − a). Not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","'Of' as an operator — a/b of Q = a/b × Q and p% of Q = (p/100) × Q. Not on the Exam Aid: remember it.","Multiplier for a p% increase = 1 + p/100 (15% gives × 1.15); for a p% decrease = 1 − p/100 (35% gives × 0.65). Not on the Exam Aid: remember both.","Decimal equivalents that save a division: 1/2 = 0.5, 1/4 = 0.25, 3/4 = 0.75, 1/5 = 0.2, 1/8 = 0.125, 3/8 = 0.375, 1/10 = 0.1, 1/20 = 0.05, 1/25 = 0.04, 1/40 = 0.025. Nowhere on the Exam Aid: remember them.","The one multiplier result the Foundation Exam Aid (W87990A) does print is Total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, for interest compounded over n periods — the repeated-multiplier case, which belongs to the growth and decay room, not to the single-step multipliers here.","Nothing else in this room is printed: the Foundation sheet gives only the area of a trapezium, the volume of a prism, the circumference and area of a circle, a² + b² = c², sin A = a/c, cos A = b/c, tan A = a/b, the compound interest formula and P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B), all of which belong to other rooms."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T11:41:22Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.93,"notes":"Answer keys, mark tallies and solution lines are all correct and the Foundation formulae-sheet claims match Exam Aid W87990A exactly — publish as it is, and only trim the hints on the 7% multiplier question if you want students to do any of it themselves."}}},"number-measures":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"number-measures","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Measures and accuracy marks are spread right across all three papers of 1MA1, and they are rarely the whole question: a speed, a density or a price per unit sits inside a ratio or proportion problem, a rounding instruction sits at the end of a calculator question, and an error interval sits inside a reasoning question about whether something will fit. Every answer in the exam has to be given to the accuracy asked for and with its unit, so the marks in this room protect marks everywhere else. Paper 1 is non-calculator, so estimation by rounding to 1 significant figure is also how you check your own arithmetic, and on Papers 2 and 3 it is how you catch a mistyped calculator key before it costs you the accuracy mark.","idea":[{"h":"Standard units and compound measures","t":"Use the standard units of mass (g, kg, tonnes), length (mm, cm, m, km), capacity (ml, litres), time (seconds, minutes, hours) and money (£ and pence), and name the unit with every answer — an answer with no unit loses the accuracy mark. A compound measure is one quantity divided by another: average speed = distance ÷ time, density = mass ÷ volume, price per unit = total price ÷ number of units. Its unit is written the same way: km/h, m/s, g/cm³, £ per litre. The two quantities must be in matching units before you divide, so 45 minutes becomes 0.75 hours and 1.79 kg becomes 1790 g. Decimal quantities are expected: 2.5 hours, not 2 hours 30 minutes, inside a division.","code":"N13"},{"h":"Estimating and checking by approximation","t":"To estimate, round every number in the calculation to 1 significant figure, then work out the easy calculation that is left, writing ≈ rather than =. The method mark is for the rounded calculation being written down, so a bare estimate scores nothing. Dividing by a number below 1 makes the answer bigger: ÷ 0.5 doubles, ÷ 0.2 multiplies by 5. Estimation is also how a calculator answer is checked — if the display reads 42.4 and the estimate is 400, a key has been mistyped. Questions often ask you to say whether an answer is sensible, so finish with a sentence comparing the estimate with the value given, not just the estimate itself.","code":"N14"},{"h":"Rounding and error intervals","t":"Rounding to a number of decimal places counts places after the point; rounding to significant figures starts counting at the first non-zero digit, so 0.04862 to 2 significant figures is 0.049. Look at the next digit along: 5 or more rounds up, 4 or less leaves the digit alone. An appropriate degree of accuracy usually means money to the nearest penny, or the same accuracy as the values you started with. A rounded measurement does not give the exact value, so the error interval says what it could have been: to the nearest unit u the interval is half a unit either side, written with ≤ at the lower end and < at the upper end because a value on the upper limit would round up. Truncation chops the digits off instead of rounding, so a value truncated to 8.3 lies in 8.3 ≤ n < 8.4.","code":"N15"},{"h":"Limits of accuracy","t":"A rounded or truncated measurement stands for every value in its interval, and the limits of accuracy are the two ends of that interval: a mass of 25 kg to the nearest kilogram has a lower limit of 24.5 kg and an upper limit of 25.5 kg, and the true mass is at least 24.5 kg but never quite 25.5 kg. Interpreting the limits means saying what they mean for the situation: the shortest a shelf could be, the most a lift could be carrying, whether a claimed value is possible at all. In a reasoning question the useful limit is usually the one that works against you — the shortest length when something has to fit, the largest load when something has a safe limit — and the conclusion has to be written down in words, with the comparison that justifies it.","code":"N16"}],"examples":[{"title":"Average speed as a compound measure","setup":"A car travels 150 km in 2.5 hours. Work out its average speed in km/h, giving the answer as a decimal quantity with its unit.","steps":["Average speed = distance ÷ time, so the calculation is 150 ÷ 2.5.","Doubling both parts clears the decimal: 150 ÷ 2.5 = 300 ÷ 5, which is the line the method mark is awarded for.","300 ÷ 5 = 60, so the average speed is 60 km/h."],"cues":["Which quantity is being shared out over which?","How could the divisor be made a whole number?","What unit belongs on a distance in km over a time in hours?"]},{"title":"Estimating to check a calculator answer","setup":"A calculator shows 39.7 × 5.13 ÷ 0.48 = 424.29 (to 2 decimal places). Estimate the value of 39.7 × 5.13 ÷ 0.48 by rounding each number to 1 significant figure, and decide whether the calculator answer is sensible.","steps":["Round each value to 1 significant figure: 39.7 ≈ 40, 5.13 ≈ 5 and 0.48 ≈ 0.5, choosing the nearest easy numbers.","The estimate is 40 × 5 ÷ 0.5; do the multiplication first, 40 × 5 = 200.","Dividing by 0.5 doubles a number, so 200 ÷ 0.5 = 400.","400 is close to the value the calculator displayed, so that answer is sensible."],"cues":["What is the first significant figure of each number?","Which part of the rounded calculation is easiest to do first?","What does dividing by a half do to a number?","How does the estimate compare with the display?"]},{"title":"Writing an error interval","setup":"A length is measured as 8 cm to the nearest centimetre. Write down the error interval for the length x, using inequality notation.","steps":["To the nearest centimetre means the value was rounded, so it lies within half a centimetre either side: the gap is 1 ÷ 2 = 0.5 cm.","Smallest value: 8 − 0.5 = 7.5 cm, and 7.5 cm itself rounds up to 8 cm, so 7.5 is included.","Largest value: 8 + 0.5 = 8.5 cm, but 8.5 cm would round up to 9 cm, so 8.5 is not included.","So the error interval is 7.5 ≤ x < 8.5, in cm."],"cues":["How far either side of the reading can the true value be?","What is the smallest length that still rounds to this reading?","Does the value at the top of the range round up or down?","Which end of the interval needs the strict inequality?"]},{"title":"Interpreting limits of accuracy","setup":"A time is measured as 12.5 seconds, correct to the nearest 0.1 second. State the limits of accuracy of the measurement, and say whether the true time could have been 12.44 seconds.","steps":["Nearest 0.1 second means the reading was rounded, so the true value lies within 0.1 ÷ 2 = 0.05 s of it.","Lower limit: 12.5 − 0.05 = 12.45 s. Upper limit: 12.5 + 0.05 = 12.55 s.","As an interval, 12.45 ≤ t < 12.55, so the true time is at least 12.45 s and below 12.55 s.","The value suggested in the question is below 12.45 s, so no — the true time could not have been that small."],"cues":["Half of which unit gives the gap either side?","What are the two ends of the range?","How is that range written in inequality notation?","Does the suggested value lie inside the range?"]},{"title":"Price per litre and best value","setup":"A 750 ml bottle of juice costs £2.19. A 2 litre carton of the same juice costs £5.60. Work out the price per litre of each, to the nearest penny, and say which is the better value.","steps":["750 ml = 750 ÷ 1000 = 0.75 litres, so the bottle gives 0.75 litres for £2.19.","Bottle: price per litre = 2.19 ÷ 0.75 = £2.92.","Carton: price per litre = 5.60 ÷ 2 = £2.80.","£2.80 per litre is less than £2.92 per litre, so the carton is the better value."],"cues":["What has to happen before the two sizes can be compared?","Which quantity is divided by which for a price per litre?","What is the same calculation for the larger container?","Which price per litre is the smaller?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A width is 4.7 cm to the nearest 0.1 cm. Write down the error interval for the width w.","a":"4.65 ≤ w < 4.75 (cm)"},{"q":"Estimate 396 ÷ 1.96 by rounding each number to 1 significant figure.","a":"200 (from 400 ÷ 2)"},{"q":"A runner covers 250 m in 50 seconds. Work out the average speed in m/s.","a":"5 m/s"},{"q":"Round 2.0649 to 2 decimal places.","a":"2.06"}]},"room":{"facts":["Average speed = distance ÷ time, so distance = speed × time and time = distance ÷ speed. Distance in km with time in hours gives km/h; metres with seconds gives m/s.","Density = mass ÷ volume (g/cm³ or kg/m³), and price per unit = total price ÷ number of units. Both quantities must be in matching units before dividing.","To estimate: round every number to 1 significant figure, then do the easy calculation, writing ≈ not =. Dividing by 0.5 doubles a number; dividing by 0.2 multiplies it by 5.","Metric units: 1 km = 1000 m, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 cm = 10 mm, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 tonne = 1000 kg, 1 litre = 1000 ml, 1 ml = 1 cm³, 1 hour = 60 minutes.","Rounding: look at the next digit along — 5 or more rounds up, 4 or less leaves the digit alone. Significant figures are counted from the first non-zero digit.","A measurement given to the nearest u has error interval (value − u/2) ≤ x < (value + u/2); a measurement truncated at that place has value ≤ x < value + u."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Round 0.04862 correct to 2 significant figures.","a":"0.049","sol":"Significant figures are counted from the first non-zero digit, so the first is 4 and the second is 8. The next digit is 6, which is 5 or more, so the 8 rounds up to 9 and the zeros holding the place value are kept: 0.049 (B1). Writing 0.05 (1 significant figure) or 0.048 scores nothing.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The zeros in front do not count as significant figures.","Find the first non-zero digit, count two digits from there, then look at the digit after them.","Your first line is to mark which digit is the second significant figure."],"codes":["N15"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A water tank holds 47 000 litres, correct to the nearest thousand litres. Write down the error interval for the volume V litres.","a":"46 500 ≤ V < 47 500","sol":"To the nearest thousand the true volume lies within half a thousand of the reading, and half of 1000 is 500. 47 000 − 500 = 46 500 and 47 000 + 500 = 47 500 (B1 for both values seen). A volume of 46 500 litres rounds up to 47 000 so it is included, while 47 500 litres rounds up to 48 000 so it is not: 46 500 ≤ V < 47 500 (B1 for the fully correct inequality).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The figure given is not exact — it stands for a whole range of volumes.","Work out half of the unit the value was rounded to, then go that far either side.","Your first line is half of the rounding unit; then take it away from, and add it to, the value."],"codes":["N15"]},{"q":"A part-time worker is paid £58.50 for 4.5 hours of work. Work out her rate of pay in £ per hour. A calculator may be used.","a":"£13 per hour","sol":"Rate of pay is a compound measure: pay ÷ time worked = 58.50 ÷ 4.5 (M1). This gives 13, so the rate of pay is £13 per hour (A1; the unit is needed for the accuracy mark).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A rate of pay measures money against the time worked.","Divide the total pay by the number of hours worked.","Your first line is total pay ÷ number of hours."],"codes":["N13"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Estimate the value of 4.8 × 19.7 by rounding each number to 1 significant figure.","a":"100","sol":"Rounding each number to 1 significant figure gives 4.8 ≈ 5 and 19.7 ≈ 20, so the calculation becomes 5 × 20 (M1 for both roundings and the product written down). 5 × 20 = 100 (A1). The exact value is 94.56, so the estimate is a sensible check.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["You are not meant to work out the exact product here.","Replace each number by the nearest number with one non-zero digit.","Your first line writes the rounded calculation, with ≈ instead of =."],"codes":["N14"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A bag of sand has a mass of 25 kg, correct to the nearest kilogram. Write down the smallest possible mass of the bag.","a":"24.5 kg","sol":"To the nearest kilogram the true mass lies within 0.5 kg either side of 25 kg, so the lower limit is 25 − 0.5 = 24.5 kg (B1, unit needed). A mass of exactly 24.5 kg rounds up to 25 kg, so it is the smallest mass possible.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The mass on the label is a rounded value, not the exact mass.","For a value rounded to the nearest kilogram, the true mass can be up to half a kilogram either side.","Your first line is half of the unit the mass was rounded to."],"codes":["N16"]},{"q":"A solid block of copper has a mass of 1.79 kg and a volume of 200 cm³. Work out the density of the copper in g/cm³. A calculator may be used.","a":"8.95 g/cm³","sol":"The unit asked for uses grams, so convert the mass first: 1.79 kg = 1.79 × 1000 = 1790 g (M1). Density = mass ÷ volume = 1790 ÷ 200 (M1). This gives 8.95, so the density is 8.95 g/cm³ (A1, unit needed).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Check the units the answer has to be in before you divide anything.","Convert the mass into grams, then divide mass by volume.","Your first line converts kilograms into grams by multiplying by 1000."],"codes":["N13"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Estimate the value of 29.3² ÷ 5.8.","a":"150","sol":"Round each number to 1 significant figure: 29.3 ≈ 30 and 5.8 ≈ 6 (M1). Square first: 30² = 900 (M1). Then 900 ÷ 6 = 150 (A1). The exact value is 148.0 to 1 decimal place, so the estimate is a good check.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Pick numbers that make both the squaring and the dividing easy.","Round both values to 1 significant figure, then follow the order of operations.","Your first line is the rounded calculation written with the square still to be worked out."],"codes":["N14"]},{"q":"A train travels 84 km in 45 minutes. Work out its average speed in km/h. A calculator may be used.","a":"112 km/h","sol":"The unit is km/h, so the time must be in hours: 45 minutes = 45 ÷ 60 = 0.75 hours (M1). Average speed = distance ÷ time = 84 ÷ 0.75 (M1). This gives 112, so the average speed is 112 km/h (A1, unit needed).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The two quantities have to be in units that match the answer.","Turn the time into a decimal number of hours, then divide distance by time.","Your first line divides the number of minutes by 60."],"codes":["N13"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A number n is truncated to 1 decimal place, giving 8.3. Write down the error interval for n.","a":"8.3 ≤ n < 8.4","sol":"Truncating chops off the digits after the first decimal place instead of rounding, so every value from 8.3 up to (but not including) 8.4 is truncated to 8.3 — for example 8.39 truncates to 8.3 (B1 for 8.3 and 8.4 identified as the limits). The lower limit is included and the upper limit is not: 8.3 ≤ n < 8.4 (B1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Truncating and rounding do not give the same interval.","Ask what the largest and smallest numbers are whose digits start this way.","Your first line writes down the two values the interval runs between."],"codes":["N15"]},{"q":"A car uses 41.6 litres of fuel to travel 508 km. Work out the fuel used per 100 km, correct to 3 significant figures. A calculator may be used.","a":"8.19 litres per 100 km","sol":"Fuel per kilometre = 41.6 ÷ 508 = 0.0818897… litres (M1). Fuel per 100 km = 0.0818897… × 100 = 8.18897… (M1). The fourth significant figure is 8, which is 5 or more, so this rounds to 8.19 litres per 100 km (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["This is a compound measure: an amount of fuel measured against a distance.","Find the fuel used for one kilometre first, then scale it up.","Your first line divides the volume of fuel by the distance travelled."],"codes":["N13","N15"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A bottle is labelled as containing 500 ml, correct to the nearest 10 ml. Dan says the bottle could contain 506 ml. Is Dan right? You must give a reason.","a":"No — the volume must be less than 505 ml","sol":"To the nearest 10 ml the true volume lies within 10 ÷ 2 = 5 ml either side, so the error interval is 495 ≤ v < 505 in ml (B1). 506 ml is above the upper limit of 505 ml, so Dan is wrong (B1 for the conclusion with the comparison).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The label is a rounded value, so the contents lie in a range.","Work out the two limits the rounding allows, then test Dan's value against them.","Your first line is half of the unit the volume was rounded to."],"codes":["N16"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A shelf is 2.4 m long, correct to the nearest 0.1 m. Ben wants to cut 8 pieces, each exactly 0.3 m long, from the shelf. Can Ben be certain that he has enough shelf? You must show your working.","a":"No — the shelf could be only 2.35 m, which is less than the 2.4 m needed","sol":"Length of shelf needed: 8 × 0.3 = 2.4 m (M1). The shelf is rounded to the nearest 0.1 m, so the lower limit of its length is 2.4 − 0.05 = 2.35 m (M1). Since 2.35 m is less than the 2.4 m needed, the shelf might be too short, so Ben cannot be certain (A1 for the comparison and the conclusion).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Two lengths have to be compared: what is needed and what is certainly there.","Work out the total length of the cuts, then the shortest the shelf could possibly be.","Your first line multiplies the number of pieces by the length of one piece."],"codes":["N16"]},{"q":"A lorry travels 210 km at an average speed of 60 km/h, and then 90 km at an average speed of 45 km/h. Work out the average speed for the whole journey, correct to 3 significant figures. A calculator may be used.","a":"54.5 km/h","sol":"First stage: time = distance ÷ speed = 210 ÷ 60 = 3.5 hours (M1). Second stage: 90 ÷ 45 = 2 hours (M1). Total distance = 210 + 90 = 300 km and total time = 3.5 + 2 = 5.5 hours, so average speed = 300 ÷ 5.5 (M1). This gives 54.545…, which is 54.5 km/h to 3 significant figures (A1). Averaging the two speeds instead gives 52.5 km/h and scores no accuracy mark.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["An average speed for a whole journey is never the average of the two speeds.","Find the time for each stage, then use the total distance and the total time.","Your first line rearranges the speed formula to time = distance ÷ speed."],"codes":["N13","N15"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A club has 312 members. Each member pays £48.50 a year. The treasurer says the yearly income will be about £20 000. Use estimation to decide whether the treasurer is right. You must show your working.","a":"No — the income is about £15 000","sol":"Round each value to 1 significant figure: 312 ≈ 300 and £48.50 ≈ £50 (M1). Estimated income = 300 × 50 (M1). 3 × 5 = 15 and there are three zeros to replace, so the estimate is £15 000 (A1 for £15 000 together with the conclusion that £20 000 is too high). The exact value is £15 132, so the estimate is a fair one.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["An estimate is enough here — the exact total is not needed.","Round the number of members and the yearly payment to 1 significant figure, then multiply.","Your first line writes the two rounded values as a product, using ≈."],"codes":["N14"]},{"q":"A gold bar is a cuboid measuring 12 cm by 6 cm by 4 cm. The density of the gold is 19.3 g/cm³. Work out the mass of the bar in kilograms, correct to 3 significant figures. A calculator may be used.","a":"5.56 kg","sol":"Volume of the cuboid = 12 × 6 × 4 = 288 cm³ (M1). Rearranging density = mass ÷ volume gives mass = density × volume = 19.3 × 288 (M1), which is 5558.4 g (A1). Converting, 5558.4 ÷ 1000 = 5.5584 kg, which is 5.56 kg to 3 significant figures (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The density tells you the mass of each cubic centimetre of gold.","Find the volume of the cuboid first, then use the density formula rearranged for mass.","Your first line multiplies the three dimensions of the cuboid together."],"codes":["N13","N15"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Round 0.008361 correct to 2 significant figures.","a":"0.0084"},{"q":"A length L is 18 cm to the nearest centimetre. Write down the error interval for L.","a":"17.5 ≤ L < 18.5"},{"q":"A cyclist covers 400 m in 50 seconds. Work out the average speed in m/s.","a":"8 m/s"},{"q":"Estimate 8.13 × 29.6 by rounding each number to 1 significant figure.","a":"240 (from 8 × 30)"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Compound measure","back":"A measure made by dividing one quantity by another, such as speed, density or price per litre. Its unit is written the same way: km/h, g/cm³, £ per litre.","code":"N13","open":false},{"front":"Average speed","back":"Average speed = distance ÷ time. Rearranged: distance = speed × time, time = distance ÷ speed. Units must match — km with hours gives km/h, metres with seconds gives m/s.","code":"N13","open":false},{"front":"Density","back":"Density = mass ÷ volume, so mass = density × volume. Measured in g/cm³ or kg/m³. Convert the mass and volume into the units the answer needs before dividing.","code":"N13","open":false},{"front":"Price per unit (unit pricing)","back":"Price per unit = total price ÷ number of units, for example £ per litre or pence per gram. The better value is the one with the smaller price per unit.","code":"N13","open":false},{"front":"Standard metric conversions","back":"1 km = 1000 m, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 cm = 10 mm, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 tonne = 1000 kg, 1 litre = 1000 ml, 1 ml = 1 cm³, £1 = 100 p.","code":"N13","open":false},{"front":"Writing a time as a decimal number of hours","back":"Divide the minutes by 60: 45 minutes = 45 ÷ 60 = 0.75 hours, 15 minutes = 0.25 hours, 20 minutes = 1/3 hour. Needed before dividing to get a speed in km/h.","code":"N13","open":false},{"front":"Estimating a calculation","back":"Round every number to 1 significant figure, then work out the easy calculation, writing ≈ not =. The rounded calculation must be written down: a bare estimate earns no method mark.","code":"N14","open":false},{"front":"Worst case of a rounded measurement","back":"The value at the wrong end of one measurement's error interval: a rope given as 5 m to the nearest metre could be only 4.5 m, so it may not reach; a 5 kg load could be nearly 5.5 kg.","code":"N16","open":false},{"front":"Give one way to check that a calculator answer is about right","back":"Estimate by rounding each value to 1 significant figure and compare; check the size of the answer against the situation (a person's mass is not 700 kg); redo the calculation with the inverse operation.","code":"N14","open":true},{"front":"Significant figures","back":"Counted from the first non-zero digit. In 0.04862 the first significant figure is 4; to 2 significant figures it is 0.049. Zeros in front only hold place value and are not significant.","code":"N15","open":false},{"front":"The rounding rule","back":"Look at the digit after the place you are rounding to: 5 or more rounds the digit up, 4 or less leaves it as it is. Digits after the rounding place are dropped, place-holding zeros kept.","code":"N15","open":false},{"front":"Error interval for a rounded measurement","back":"A value x given as a to the nearest u lies in (a − u/2) ≤ x < (a + u/2). A length of 8 cm to the nearest cm gives 7.5 ≤ x < 8.5.","code":"N15","open":false},{"front":"Truncation","back":"Chopping off the later digits instead of rounding. A number truncated to 8.3 lies in 8.3 ≤ n < 8.4, so the stated value is always the lower limit of the interval.","code":"N15","open":false},{"front":"Appropriate degree of accuracy","back":"Money to the nearest penny (2 decimal places), and other answers to about the accuracy of the values given, usually 3 significant figures from a calculator. Never round part-way through a calculation.","code":"N15","open":false},{"front":"Limits of accuracy","back":"The values a single rounded measurement lies between, read off its error interval: a mass of 25 kg to the nearest kilogram lies in 24.5 ≤ m < 25.5, so it is at least 24.5 kg and under 25.5 kg.","code":"N16","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Preamble: the Exam Aid formulae sheet printed as an insert with every Foundation tier 1MA1 paper gives only the eight results listed next, and none of them belongs to this room, so every result marked Remember must be written from memory.","Given on the Exam Aid: area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h, where a and b are the parallel sides and h their perpendicular separation.","Given on the Exam Aid: volume of a prism = area of cross section × length.","Given on the Exam Aid: circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd, where r is the radius and d the diameter.","Given on the Exam Aid: area of a circle = πr².","Given on the Exam Aid: Pythagoras' theorem a² + b² = c², in a right-angled triangle whose hypotenuse is c.","Given on the Exam Aid: sin A = a/c, cos A = b/c, tan A = a/b, in a right-angled triangle ABC whose hypotenuse is c.","Given on the Exam Aid: compound interest total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, where P is the principal, r the interest rate per period and n the number of periods.","Given on the Exam Aid: P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B).","Remember: average speed = distance ÷ time — not given on the Exam Aid, which prints no compound measure.","Remember: distance = speed × time and time = distance ÷ speed, the two rearrangements of the speed formula.","Remember: density = mass ÷ volume, so mass = density × volume and volume = mass ÷ density.","Remember: price per unit = total price ÷ number of units; the better value is the smaller price per unit.","Remember: to estimate, round every number to 1 significant figure and work out the easy calculation, writing ≈ not =.","Remember: a value a given to the nearest u has error interval (a − u/2) ≤ x < (a + u/2), with ≤ at the lower end and < at the upper end.","Remember: a value a truncated at a place value of u has interval a ≤ x < a + u, so the value given is the lower limit.","Remember: 1 km = 1000 m, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 cm = 10 mm, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 tonne = 1000 kg, 1 litre = 1000 ml, 1 ml = 1 cm³.","Remember: minutes become a decimal number of hours by dividing by 60, so 45 minutes = 0.75 hours.","Remember: every answer in this room carries its unit — km/h, g/cm³, £ per litre, cm — because the accuracy mark depends on it."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T11:40:27Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"Answer keys, solutions, marks and units are sound throughout — all 15 bank questions, both four-item checklists and all five worked examples re-solved and confirmed, nine questions correctly flagged non-calculator with hand-workable numbers, and the Formulae sheet matches the 2026 Foundation Exam Aid exactly — so it is publishable as it stands; only the discrete-population interval in question 1 and the packed first formulae item are worth a teacher's pencil."}}},"algebra-notation":{"id":"EDX-1MA1","topic":"algebra-notation","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Every other piece of algebra you meet is written in the notation of this room. Solving an equation, using a formula in a mensuration or ratio question, reading a straight-line graph or testing a sequence rule all assume you can write a × b as ab, put a negative value in brackets before squaring it, tell an expression from an equation, an identity from a formula, collect like terms, expand and factorise, and turn a formula round to give a different letter. Marks here are lost far more often to careless notation — 0.25x written where x/4 is wanted, −4² worked out as though it were (−4)², a minus sign dropped after a bracket — than to any hard idea, and much of it is examined without a calculator, where the written method is what earns the method mark.","idea":[{"h":"The conventions of algebraic notation","t":"Algebra has a shorthand and examiners expect it. A multiplication sign is left out, so a × b is written ab and 3 × y is written 3y; repeated addition of the same letter is written as a multiple, so y + y + y is 3y; repeated multiplication is written as a power, so a × a is a², a × a × a is a³ and a × a × b is a²b. A division is written as a fraction: a ÷ b is a/b. The number multiplying a letter is its coefficient, and it is written as a fraction rather than a decimal, so x/4, never 0.25x. Brackets mean that everything inside is treated as one quantity first, so 3(x + 2) is three lots of the whole of x + 2. Questions ask you to write an expression in this form, and every later answer is marked in it.","code":"A1"},{"h":"Substituting numerical values","t":"To find a value from a formula or an expression, replace each letter by the number it stands for and then work the arithmetic out in the usual priority: brackets, then powers, then × and ÷, then + and −. The trap is negative values: write each one in brackets as you substitute, because (−4)² = 16 while −4² means −(4²) = −16. Scientific formulae such as v = u + at are substituted the same way, and the answer is given with its unit and to the accuracy asked for. The method mark is for the substitution written down, so put the line with the numbers in it on the page before you evaluate anything.","code":"A2"},{"h":"Expression, equation, formula, identity, inequality","t":"These five words are examined directly. An expression, such as 5y − 2, has no equals sign; it can be simplified or evaluated but not solved. An equation, such as 5y − 2 = 13, has an equals sign and is true only for particular values. An identity, written with ≡, is true for every value: 2(x + 3) ≡ 2x + 6. A formula, such as A = πr² or v = u + at, gives a rule for working out one quantity, its subject, from others. An inequality, such as y > 2, compares two expressions with <, ≤, > or ≥. Within an expression, a term is a part separated by + or −, and a factor is something the expression divides by exactly.","code":"A3"},{"h":"Simplifying and manipulating expressions","t":"Four techniques do most of the work. Collect like terms: only terms with exactly the same letters and powers can be joined, and each term keeps the sign in front of it. Multiply a single term over a bracket: every term inside is multiplied, signs included. Take out common factors: divide each term by the highest common factor and write it outside the bracket, and check by expanding. Expand products of two binomials: every term in the first bracket multiplies every term in the second, giving four products to collect. The same statement covers factorising x² + bx + c, where the two numbers multiply to c and add to b, the difference of two squares x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a), and the laws of indices: xᵃ × xᵇ = xᵃ⁺ᵇ, xᵃ ÷ xᵇ = xᵃ⁻ᵇ, (xᵃ)ᵇ = xᵃᵇ and x⁰ = 1. 'Simplify fully' and 'factorise fully' mean a partly finished answer scores no accuracy mark.","code":"A4"},{"h":"Standard formulae and changing the subject","t":"A formula has a subject — the letter standing alone on one side — and you are often asked for a different one. Work out what has been done to the letter you want, then undo those operations in reverse order, doing the same thing to both sides each time, until that letter stands alone and appears only once. To make t the subject of v = u + at, take u from both sides and then divide by a; to make r the subject of A = πr², divide by π and then take the square root. Standard formulae such as the area of a circle or a trapezium are printed on the Exam Aid, so the marks are for the rearranging and the substituting, not for the recall.","code":"A5"},{"h":"Showing two expressions are always equal","t":"An equation is true for particular values of the letter; an identity is true for all of them. To show two expressions are equivalent, take one side, expand every bracket and collect every like term, and show the result is exactly the other side — then say so. That single sentence is what turns working into an argument, and it is where the reasoning mark sits. Testing one value is not enough: it shows the two sides agree there, not everywhere. Once shown, the statement may be written with ≡ instead of =. The same expand-and-collect chain proves results about numbers, such as showing an expression is always even or always a multiple of 5.","code":"A6"},{"h":"An expression as a function: input and output","t":"An expression such as 3x − 1 can be read as a machine: an input x goes in, it is multiplied by 3 and then 1 is taken away, and an output comes out. Substituting an input gives the output. Running the machine backwards — undoing each operation in reverse order — takes an output back to the input that produced it, which is the same inverse-operation thinking used to change the subject of a formula. Questions give a function machine, a mapping written x → 3x − 1, or a plain expression, and ask for an output from a given input or the input that gives a stated output.","code":"A7"}],"examples":[{"title":"Substituting a negative value into a scientific formula","setup":"The kinetic energy of a moving object is E = ½mv², where m is its mass in kilograms and v is its velocity in m/s. A trolley of mass m = 6 kg is rolling backwards, so its velocity is v = −4 m/s. Work out the value of E, in joules.","steps":["Replace each letter by its value, keeping the negative one in brackets: E = ½ × 6 × (−4)²","Powers are worked out before multiplying, and (−4)² = −4 × −4 = 16","Now multiply from the left: ½ × 6 = 3, so E = 3 × 16","E = 48 J"],"cues":["Why must the velocity be written inside brackets here?","Which part of this calculation is done first?","What is left to work out once the square is known?","What unit must be attached to the value?"]},{"title":"Expanding and simplifying a product of two brackets","setup":"Expand and simplify the product (2x + 5)(x − 4), writing the answer as a quadratic expression with its terms in descending powers of x.","steps":["Every term in the left bracket multiplies every term in the right: 2x × x = 2x², 2x × (−4) = −8x, 5 × x = 5x, 5 × (−4) = −20","Written in a row, the four products give 2x² − 8x + 5x − 20","The x terms are like terms and combine: −8x + 5x = −3x","So (2x + 5)(x − 4) = 2x² − 3x − 20"],"cues":["How many separate products will multiplying out give?","Which of the four products are negative?","Which two of the four terms can be joined together?","Will the coefficient of x end up positive or negative?"]},{"title":"Changing the subject of a perimeter formula","setup":"The perimeter P of a rectangle of length l and width w is given by the formula P = 2(l + w). Rearrange this formula to make w the subject.","steps":["The bracket is multiplied by 2 last, so undo that first: divide both sides by 2 to get P/2 = l + w","The length is added to w, so subtract l from both sides: P/2 − l = w","Turn the formula round so the subject is written first: w = P/2 − l"],"cues":["Which operation is applied to the bracket last?","What is still attached to w after the first move?","On which side should the new subject be written?"]},{"title":"Deciding whether a statement is an identity","setup":"Priya says that 5(x + 2) − 3(x − 1) is the same as 2x + 13 for every value of x. Show whether Priya is right, and say whether her statement is an equation or an identity.","steps":["Multiply out the left bracket: 5(x + 2) = 5x + 10","Multiply out the right bracket, remembering the sign in front of it: −3(x − 1) = −3x + 3","Collect like terms: 5x − 3x = 2x and 10 + 3 = 13, so the whole expression is 2x + 13","That is exactly the expression Priya gave, and it came from simplifying, not from choosing a value, so she is right for every value of x","A statement true for every value is an identity, written 5(x + 2) − 3(x − 1) ≡ 2x + 13"],"cues":["What must happen to both brackets before anything can be compared?","What sign will the number term from the second bracket have?","Which terms in the four can be joined together?","Why is testing x = 1 not enough to settle this?","Which word describes a statement true for all values?"]},{"title":"Factorising fully by taking out the common factor","setup":"Factorise fully 6a²b + 15ab², giving your answer as a product of a single term and a bracket.","steps":["The highest common factor of 6 and 15 is 3, and both terms contain at least one a and one b, so the common factor is 3ab","Divide each term by that factor to find what goes inside the bracket: 6a²b ÷ 3ab = 2a and 15ab² ÷ 3ab = 5b","Write the common factor outside and the results inside: 6a²b + 15ab² = 3ab(2a + 5b)","Expanding checks it: 3ab × 2a = 6a²b and 3ab × 5b = 15ab², so the factorisation is right and nothing is left to take out"],"cues":["What number and what letters divide into both terms?","How do you find what belongs inside the bracket?","Where does the common factor go in the answer?","How can a factorisation be checked in one line?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Write 7 × y × y × y in conventional algebraic notation.","a":"7y³"},{"q":"Work out the value of 3a − b² when a = 5 and b = −3.","a":"6"},{"q":"Is 4(x + 1) = 4x + 4 an equation or an identity? Give the reason.","a":"An identity — both sides simplify to 4x + 4, so it is true for every value of x"},{"q":"Make h the subject of the formula A = ½bh.","a":"h = 2A/b"},{"q":"Simplify 8c + 5d − 3c − 9d.","a":"5c − 4d"}]},"room":{"facts":["The notation conventions: ab means a × b; 3y means y + y + y and 3 × y; a² means a × a; a²b means a × a × b; a/b means a ÷ b; brackets group a whole quantity.","A coefficient is written as a fraction, not a decimal: x/4, never 0.25x.","Substitution order: brackets, then powers, then × and ÷, then + and −, with every negative value put in brackets, so (−4)² = 16 but −4² = −16.","An expression has no equals sign; an equation is true for particular values; an identity (≡) is true for all values; a formula gives a rule for its subject; an inequality compares with <, ≤, > or ≥.","Laws of indices: xᵃ × xᵇ = xᵃ⁺ᵇ; xᵃ ÷ xᵇ = xᵃ⁻ᵇ; (xᵃ)ᵇ = xᵃᵇ; x⁰ = 1.","x² + bx + c = (x + p)(x + q) where p × q = c and p + q = b; the difference of two squares is x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a).","To change the subject, apply the inverse operation to both sides in turn until the wanted letter stands alone on one side and appears only once."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Write each of these in conventional algebraic notation: (i) t + t + t + t, (ii) m × m × n.","a":"(i) 4t (ii) m²n","sol":"(i) Four lots of t is 4 × t, and the multiplication sign is left out with the number written in front: 4t (B1). (ii) m × m is written as a power, m², and the remaining letter is written next to it with no sign: m²n (B1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Repeated addition of one letter, and repeated multiplication of one letter, each have a shorter standard form.","Count how many lots of the letter the first one has, and how many times the letter multiplies itself in the second.","Write the first as a number in front of t with the sign left out, and the second as a power of m written next to n."],"codes":["A1"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Write a ÷ b in conventional algebraic notation, and rewrite the expression 0.25x so that its coefficient is written as a fraction.","a":"a/b and x/4","sol":"A division is written as a fraction with the number being divided on top: a ÷ b = a/b (B1). A coefficient is written as a fraction rather than a decimal, and 0.25 = 25/100 = 1/4, so 0.25x is written x/4 (B1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Divisions and decimal coefficients each have a preferred written form in algebra.","A fraction bar does the same job as ÷, and a decimal coefficient is the same as a fraction of the letter.","Write the division with a horizontal bar, and convert the decimal to a fraction in its lowest terms before attaching the letter."],"codes":["A1"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Here are four statements: 5y − 2, 5y − 2 = 13, C = 5y, y > 2. Write down which one is a formula, and write down what kind of statement 5y − 2 = 13 is.","a":"C = 5y is the formula; 5y − 2 = 13 is an equation","sol":"A formula gives a rule for working out one named quantity from another, so it has a subject standing alone on one side: C = 5y (B1). 5y − 2 = 13 has an equals sign but is true only for the particular value y = 3, so it is an equation, not an identity (B1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Look at the signs first: two of the four have an equals sign, one has an inequality sign and one has neither.","A formula works out one named quantity from others; an equation with an equals sign holds only for particular values.","Ask of each statement in turn whether it has an equals sign, and if it does, whether it tells you how to work out a named quantity."],"codes":["A3"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Simplify 7a + 4b − 3a + 2b.","a":"4a + 6b","sol":"Collect the a terms, keeping the sign in front of each: 7a − 3a = 4a (B1). Collect the b terms: 4b + 2b = 6b (B1). The two results are unlike terms, so the answer is left as 4a + 6b. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Only terms with exactly the same letter can be joined together.","Deal with the a terms and the b terms separately, and keep the sign that sits in front of each term with it.","Rewrite the expression with the a terms next to each other and the b terms next to each other before combining anything."],"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"(calculator allowed) The formula v = u + at gives the velocity v, in m/s, of an object t seconds after it starts. Work out v when u = 3.2 m/s, a = 1.5 m/s² and t = 4 s.","a":"9.2 m/s","sol":"Replace each letter by its value: v = 3.2 + 1.5 × 4 (M1). Multiplication comes before addition, so 1.5 × 4 = 6 and v = 3.2 + 6 = 9.2 m/s (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Each letter in the formula stands for one of the values listed in the question.","Once the values are in place, the priority of operations decides which part is worked out first.","Write the formula out with each letter replaced by its value, then evaluate the product before the addition."],"codes":["A2"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A function takes an input x and gives the output 4x − 5. Write down the output when the input is 6, and the output when the input is −1.","a":"19 and −9","sol":"Substitute x = 6: 4 × 6 − 5 = 24 − 5 = 19 (B1). Substitute x = −1, keeping the negative input in brackets: 4 × (−1) − 5 = −4 − 5 = −9 (B1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The expression works like a machine: whatever goes in is multiplied, then something is taken away.","Put each input in place of x and carry out the two operations in the order the expression sets.","Write 4 × (the input) − 5 for each of the two inputs, putting a negative input in brackets."],"codes":["A7","A2"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A rectangle has length (x + 7) cm and width (x − 2) cm. Write an expression, in its simplest form, for the area of the rectangle in cm².","a":"x² + 5x − 14","sol":"Area = length × width = (x + 7)(x − 2). Every term in the first bracket multiplies every term in the second: x² − 2x + 7x − 14 (M1 for at least three of the four products correct). Collect the like terms, −2x + 7x = 5x, giving an area of x² + 5x − 14 cm² (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The area of a rectangle is worked out the same way whether its sides are numbers or expressions.","Multiplying two brackets means each term of one multiplies each term of the other, giving four products to collect.","Write the area as the product of the two bracketed sides, then multiply them out term by term before collecting."],"codes":["A4","A1"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Factorise x² + 7x + 12.","a":"(x + 3)(x + 4)","sol":"The expression factorises as (x + p)(x + q) where p × q = 12 and p + q = 7. The pairs of factors of 12 are 1 and 12, 2 and 6, 3 and 4, and only the last pair adds to 7 (M1). So x² + 7x + 12 = (x + 3)(x + 4) (A1), which expanding confirms. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["A quadratic in this form comes from multiplying two brackets, each beginning with x.","The two numbers in the brackets multiply to give the number term and add to give the coefficient of x.","List the pairs of whole numbers whose product is the number term, then test which pair has the right sum."],"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Simplify (p⁵ × p⁴) ÷ p³.","a":"p⁶","sol":"Multiplying powers of the same letter adds the indices: p⁵ × p⁴ = p⁵⁺⁴ = p⁹ (M1). Dividing subtracts them: p⁹ ÷ p³ = p⁹⁻³ = p⁶ (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["All three powers are powers of the same letter, so the laws of indices apply throughout.","One law handles the multiplication and a second handles the division; work the bracket out first.","Add the indices of the two powers being multiplied, then subtract the index of the power being divided by."],"codes":["A4"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out the value of 5x² − 3y when x = −3 and y = −2.","a":"51","sol":"Substitute, keeping each negative value in brackets: 5 × (−3)² − 3 × (−2). Powers first: (−3)² = 9, so the first term is 5 × 9 = 45, and the second is −3 × (−2) = +6 (M1 for either term correct). Adding them: 45 + 6 = 51 (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["A squared negative number and a subtracted negative number both need care in this one.","Replace the letters first, with brackets round each negative value, then follow the priority of operations.","Write 5 × (the x value in brackets)² − 3 × (the y value in brackets) before working anything out."],"codes":["A2"]},{"q":"(calculator allowed) Make t the subject of the formula v = u + at.","a":"t = (v − u)/a","sol":"The term at is added to u, so subtract u from both sides: v − u = at (M1). t is multiplied by a, so divide both sides by a: (v − u)/a = t (A1). Written with the subject first, t = (v − u)/a. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The letter you want is inside a product that is being added to another letter.","Undo the operations in the reverse of the order they were applied, doing the same to both sides each time.","Take the letter that is added to the at term away from both sides first, then deal with what multiplies t."],"codes":["A5"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Show that 3(2y − 1) + 5 is equal to 6y + 2 for every value of y, and name the kind of statement this makes.","a":"3(2y − 1) + 5 = 6y − 3 + 5 = 6y + 2, so the statement is an identity","sol":"Expand the bracket: 3 × 2y = 6y and 3 × (−1) = −3, giving 6y − 3 + 5 (M1). Collect the number terms, −3 + 5 = 2, so the left-hand side simplifies to 6y + 2, which is exactly the right-hand side (A1). The two sides agree for every value of y, not just particular ones, so the statement is an identity and may be written 3(2y − 1) + 5 ≡ 6y + 2 (B1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Showing two expressions are always equal means simplifying one of them until it matches the other.","Multiply the bracket out, collect the number terms, and compare the result with the expression on the right.","Multiply each term inside the bracket by the number in front of it, leaving the + 5 where it stands."],"codes":["A6","A4","A3"]},{"q":"(calculator allowed) The formula C = 5/9 (F − 32) changes a temperature F, in degrees Fahrenheit, into the temperature C, in degrees Celsius. Rearrange the formula to make F the subject, and use it to work out F when C = 25.","a":"F = 9C/5 + 32, and F = 77","sol":"The bracket is multiplied by 5/9, so multiply both sides by its reciprocal 9/5: 9C/5 = F − 32 (M1). Add 32 to both sides: F = 9C/5 + 32 (A1). Substitute C = 25: F = 9 × 25 ÷ 5 + 32 = 45 + 32 (M1). F = 77 (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["F sits inside a bracket that is being multiplied by a fraction, so that fraction has to come off first.","Undo the multiplication by the fraction by multiplying both sides by its reciprocal, then undo the subtraction inside the bracket.","Multiply both sides by 9/5 to clear the fraction, then deal with the number that is being subtracted from F."],"codes":["A5","A2"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The sides of a triangle are (3x + 1) cm, (2x − 3) cm and (x + 5) cm. Write an expression in its simplest form for the perimeter of the triangle, and work out the perimeter when x = 4.","a":"Perimeter = 6x + 3; when x = 4 the perimeter is 27 cm","sol":"The perimeter is the sum of the three sides: (3x + 1) + (2x − 3) + (x + 5) (M1). Collect the x terms, 3x + 2x + x = 6x, and the number terms, 1 − 3 + 5 = 3, giving 6x + 3 (A1). Substitute x = 4 into the simplified expression: 6 × 4 + 3 (M1). This gives 24 + 3 = 27 cm (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A perimeter is found in the same way whether the sides are numbers or expressions.","Add the three side expressions and collect like terms, then substitute the given value into the simplified expression.","Write the three side expressions in a row joined by + signs, then group the x terms and the number terms."],"codes":["A4","A2","A1"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Priya adds 3(2n + 1) to 4(n − 3), where n is a whole number. Show that her result is always an odd number.","a":"The sum is 10n − 9, which is always odd because 10n is always even and an even number minus an odd number is odd","sol":"Expand both brackets: 3(2n + 1) = 6n + 3 and 4(n − 3) = 4n − 12 (M1). Add them and collect like terms: 6n + 4n = 10n and 3 − 12 = −9, so the sum is 10n − 9 (A1). 10n is a multiple of 10, so it is even for every whole number n (B1). An even number take away an odd number is odd, so 10n − 9 is odd whatever whole number n is (B1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Nothing can be decided while the brackets are still there, so the two expressions must first become one.","Expand and collect, then look at whether each part of the result is always even or always odd.","Multiply out each bracket by the number in front of it and write the four terms in a row before collecting."],"codes":["A6","A4"]},{"q":"(calculator allowed) The area of a circle is given by A = πr², where r is the radius. Make r the subject of this formula, and use it to find the radius of a circle of area 120 cm², giving your answer to 1 decimal place.","a":"r = √(A/π), and r = 6.2 cm","sol":"Divide both sides by π to leave the square on its own: A/π = r² (M1). Take the positive square root of both sides, since a radius is a length: r = √(A/π) (A1). Substitute A = 120: r = √(120 ÷ π) = √38.197… (M1). r = 6.180… = 6.2 cm to 1 decimal place (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The area formula itself is given to you, so the work is in turning it round and then substituting.","Undo the multiplication by π first, and then undo the squaring by the inverse operation.","Divide both sides by π so that r² stands alone, then apply the inverse of squaring to both sides."],"codes":["A5","A2"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write 4 × p × p × q in conventional algebraic notation.","a":"4p²q"},{"q":"Work out the value of 2x² + y when x = −5 and y = 3.","a":"53"},{"q":"Factorise x² − 36.","a":"(x + 6)(x − 6)"},{"q":"Make b the subject of the formula P = a + 2b.","a":"b = (P − a)/2"}]},"cards":[{"front":"The conventions of algebraic notation","back":"ab means a × b; 3y means y + y + y and 3 × y; a² means a × a; a²b means a × a × b; a/b means a ÷ b.","code":"A1","open":false},{"front":"Coefficient","back":"The number multiplying a letter in a term: in 7x it is 7, in −3x² it is −3. A coefficient is written as a fraction, x/4, not as a decimal, 0.25x.","code":"A1","open":false},{"front":"Term (in an algebraic expression)","back":"A part of an expression separated from the next by + or −. In 5x² − 3x + 7 the terms are 5x², −3x and 7.","code":"A3","open":false},{"front":"Factor of an expression","back":"Something the expression divides by exactly. 3 and (x + 2) are factors of 3x + 6, because 3x + 6 = 3(x + 2).","code":"A3","open":false},{"front":"Expression","back":"A collection of terms with no equals sign, such as 4x − 9. It can be simplified or evaluated, but it cannot be solved.","code":"A3","open":false},{"front":"Equation","back":"A statement with an equals sign that is true only for particular values, such as 3x + 1 = 7, which holds only when x = 2.","code":"A3","open":false},{"front":"Identity","back":"A statement true for every value of the letter, written with ≡, such as 2(x + 3) ≡ 2x + 6. Both sides simplify to the same expression.","code":"A6","open":false},{"front":"Formula","back":"A rule connecting quantities, with its subject standing alone on one side, such as A = πr² or v = u + at. Substituting the other letters gives the subject's value.","code":"A5","open":false},{"front":"Inequality","back":"A statement comparing two expressions with <, ≤, > or ≥, such as y > 2, which is true for a range of values rather than for one.","code":"A3","open":false},{"front":"Priority of operations when substituting","back":"Brackets, then powers, then × and ÷, then + and −. Write a negative value in brackets first: (−4)² = 16, but −4² = −16.","code":"A2","open":false},{"front":"Laws of indices","back":"xᵃ × xᵇ = xᵃ⁺ᵇ; xᵃ ÷ xᵇ = xᵃ⁻ᵇ; (xᵃ)ᵇ = xᵃᵇ; x⁰ = 1.","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Difference of two squares","back":"x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a). So x² − 25 factorises as (x + 5)(x − 5).","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Factorising x² + bx + c","back":"Find two numbers with product c and sum b, then write (x + p)(x + q). For x² + 9x + 20 the numbers are 4 and 5.","code":"A4","open":false},{"front":"Changing the subject of a formula","back":"Apply the inverse operation to both sides in turn until the wanted letter stands alone on one side and appears only once.","code":"A5","open":false},{"front":"An expression read as a function","back":"3x − 1 acts as a machine: an input x gives an output. Substituting finds the output; undoing the operations in reverse traces an output back to its input.","code":"A7","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Preamble: none of this room's own notation, index and factorising results is printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid (W87990A), the insert in every Foundation paper, so every item below marked Remember must be written from memory; the items marked Printed are given to you on that sheet, so a question that substitutes into one of them, or rearranges it, prints it for you.","Remember: ab means a × b — the multiplication sign is left out. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: 3y means y + y + y, and also 3 × y. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: a² means a × a. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: a³ means a × a × a. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: a²b means a × a × b. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: a/b means a ÷ b — a division is written as a fraction. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: a coefficient is written as a fraction, x/4, never as a decimal, 0.25x. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: brackets hold a whole quantity together, so 3(x + 2) means three lots of the whole of x + 2. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: the priority of operations when you substitute — brackets, then powers, then × and ÷, then + and −. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: put a negative value in brackets when you substitute it, so that (−4)² = 16 while −4² = −16. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: xᵃ × xᵇ = xᵃ⁺ᵇ. The Foundation sheet says nothing about the laws of indices, so remember it.","Remember: xᵃ ÷ xᵇ = xᵃ⁻ᵇ. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: (xᵃ)ᵇ = xᵃᵇ. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: x⁰ = 1. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: x² + bx + c = (x + p)(x + q), where p × q = c and p + q = b. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: the difference of two squares, x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a). Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: to change the subject, apply the inverse operation to both sides in turn until the wanted letter stands alone and appears only once. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Remember: area of a triangle = ½ × base × height, the formula behind A = ½bh. Not on the sheet, so remember it.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h, where a and b are the parallel sides and h their perpendicular separation.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: volume of a prism = area of cross section × length.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd, where r is the radius and d the diameter.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: area of a circle = πr², the formula this room's stretch question asks you to rearrange.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: Pythagoras' theorem a² + b² = c², in a right-angled triangle whose hypotenuse is c.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: sin A = a/c, in a right-angled triangle ABC whose hypotenuse is c and whose side a is opposite angle A.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: cos A = b/c, where b is the side adjacent to angle A and c the hypotenuse.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: tan A = a/b, where a is opposite angle A and b adjacent to it.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, where P is the principal, r the interest rate per period and n the number of periods.","Printed on the Foundation Tier Exam Aid: P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B)."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-15T11:43:19.036509+00:00","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.86,"notes":"Every answer key, solution line and mark tally checks out (all 16 bank questions, the 4 exit questions, the 5 lesson checks and all 5 worked examples re-solved in sympy), A1–A7 are covered in order with nothing beyond Foundation — the only repairs needed are to the wording of the Formulae sheet extra."}}}} };
